The 1929–30 Scottish football season was [[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]]'s 42nd season of competitive football, in which they competed in the [[Scottish First Division]]<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.londonhearts.com/scores/a1930/celtic1930.htm|title=Results for Celtic for 1929-30|website=Londonhearts.com|accessdate=9 August 2018}}</ref> and the [[Scottish Cup]].

It was Celtic's third consecutive season without a major domestic honour, a dry spell the club had not known since the early 1900s. They finished the league a distant fourth to [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]]. In the Scottish Cup, they saw defeat to [[St Mirren F.C.|St. Mirren]] in the third round, 1-3 at Celtic Park.

This season was [[Jimmy McStay]]'s first as Celtic captain, immediately succeeding his older brother [[Willie McStay (footballer, born 1892)|Willie]]. McStay, who had been playing for Celtic since 1920, would later be club manager from 1940 to 1945.

